// EXPECT_STDOUT a,b 2
//
// №TBD regression pin (item 32 of the crew-harness spike index
// `spikes/env/README.md` -- `repro_text_import`; real-world carrier: the
// CrewHarness session server, `acp/client.nv` arm
// `RpcGarbage { text } => text.byte_len()`).
//
// `import std.text` in ONE module of a program used to make a variable named
// `text`, bound by a RECORD pattern (`Garbage { text } => ...`) in ANOTHER
// module, mean the MODULE: `[E7401] no function `byte_len` in module `text``.
// Root cause: `match_arm_bindings` (checker) had no arm for `Pattern::Record`
// (struct-style variant / record destructure), so the arm body ran with the
// binding REMOVED from the scope (`shadow_bound_names` erases the outer
// namesake and nothing re-typed it); `f1_check_call`'s
// `scope.contains_key(prefix)` miss then classified `text.byte_len()` as a
// module-qualified call, because `imported_modules` is a WHOLE-CU set -- the
// neighbor's import registers the prefix `text` for every module of the
// program. A parameter named `text` was never affected (parameters are always
// in scope).
//
// Mode note: under `nova test` the released (pre-fix) toolchain PASSES this
// fixture silently -- the test-mode sig-table pre-pass knows `byte_len`
// (`is_known_fn` suppresses the false E7401) and codegen's own
// `var_types`-guarded module-alias rewrite dispatches the instance method
// anyway, so the wrong classification is invisible there. The defect lives in
// `nova build` (exactly how the harness hit it): on the released toolchain
//   nova build spec_tests/conformance/standalone/text_import_pattern_bind.nv
// fails `[E7401] no function `byte_len` in module `text``; with the fix it
// builds and prints the EXPECT_STDOUT line below.
//
// Shape mirrors the spike: the ENTRY module declares the sum + the record
// pattern and imports NO std module; the SIDE module imports `std.text` for
// `[]str.join`.
module standalone.text_import_pattern_bind

import ./text_import_pattern_bind_side.{joined}

type Line enum
    | Garbage { text str }
    | Empty

fn length_of(line Line) -> int {
    match line {
        Garbage { text } => text.byte_len()
        Empty => 0
    }
}

fn main() {
    println("${joined(["a", "b"])} ${length_of(Garbage { text: "xy" })}")
}
